package Plack::Middleware::OIDC;
use 5.020;
use utf8;
use strict;
use warnings;
use parent 'Plack::Middleware';

use Carp qw(croak);
use Clone qw(clone);
use Scalar::Util qw(blessed);
use Try::Tiny;
use URI ();
use OIDC::Client;
use OIDC::Client::Plugin;
use OIDC::Client::Error;
use Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Redirect;
use Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Logger;
use Plack::Request;
use Plack::Util::Accessor qw(config log);

# VERSION: generated by Dist::Zilla::Plugin::OurPkgVersion

=encoding utf8

=head1 NAME

Plack::Middleware::OIDC - OIDC protocol integration for PSGI/Plack applications

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  use Plack::Builder;

  my $oidc_config = {
    default_provider          => 'my_provider',
    authentication_error_path => '/error',
    provider => {
      my_provider => {
        id                    => 'my-app-id',
        secret                => 'xxxxxxxxx',
        well_known_url        => 'https://yourprovider.com/.well-known/openid-configuration',
        signin_redirect_path  => '/oidc/login/callback',
        logout_redirect_path  => '/oidc/logout/callback',
        scope                 => 'openid profile roles email',
      },
    },
  };

  builder {
    enable 'Session';
    enable 'OIDC', config => $oidc_config;
    $app;
  };

  # in the wrapped application :
  sub {
    my $env = shift;

    if (my $identity = $env->{oidc}->get_valid_identity()) {
      # authenticated, $identity->subject, $identity->claims, ...
    }
    else {
      # this dies with an internal exception that Plack::Middleware::OIDC
      # catches and turns into a real 302 response
      $env->{oidc}->redirect_to_authorize();
    }
  };

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This middleware makes it easy to integrate the OpenID Connect protocol
into a PSGI/Plack application. It enables applications to act as an
OpenID Connect Relying Party (RP) for user authentication and as an
OAuth 2.0 Resource Server by validating Access Tokens issued by an
external Authorization Server or OpenID Provider. It does not implement
an OpenID Provider.

It essentially uses the L<OIDC::Client|https://metacpan.org/dist/OIDC-Client>
distribution, and is the PSGI/Plack counterpart of L<Mojolicious::Plugin::OIDC>,
L<Catalyst::Plugin::OIDC> and L<Dancer2::Plugin::OIDC>. This documentation
mainly focuses on what is specific to Plack ; please also read
L<OIDC::Client::Plugin> and L<OIDC::Client::Config> for the complete
list of available methods and configuration entries.

=head1 HOW IT WORKS

=head2 Configuration

Unlike the other framework plugins, Plack has no built-in configuration
mechanism, so the configuration hashref (same structure as for the other
plugins, see L<OIDC::Client::Config>) must be passed explicitly to
C<enable>, under the C<config> key.

=head2 Routing

At startup, for each configured provider, the middleware reads the
C<signin_redirect_path> and C<logout_redirect_path> configuration entries
and intercepts requests matching these paths directly, without delegating
them to the wrapped application. There is no need to declare these routes
in your application.

=head2 Access to OIDC::Client::Plugin instances

For every other request, this middleware creates one L<OIDC::Client::Plugin>
instance per configured provider and exposes them to the downstream
application through C<$env> :

=over 2

=item C<< $env->{oidc} >>

Instance for the default provider (see L</"default_provider"> below).

=item C<< $env->{"oidc.$provider_name"} >>

Instance dedicated to the C<$provider_name> provider. When
C<$provider_name> is the default provider, this is the very same instance
as C<< $env->{oidc} >>.

=back

This is the PSGI idiomatic equivalent of the C<< $c->oidc >> /
C<< $c->oidc('my_provider') >> helper found in the other plugins.

=head2 default_provider

If only one provider is configured, it is automatically the default one.
If several providers are configured, the C<default_provider> configuration
entry must be set for C<< $env->{oidc} >> to be populated ; otherwise, only
the C<< $env->{"oidc.$provider_name"} >> keys are set.

=head2 Redirects from application code

Some methods of L<OIDC::Client::Plugin>, namely C<redirect_to_authorize()>
and C<redirect_to_logout()>, are meant to immediately redirect the
browser. Because a PSGI application has no mutable response object to
alter from arbitrary application code, calling one of these methods
actually throws an internal L<Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Redirect>
exception. This middleware catches it around the call to the downstream
application and turns it into a real C<302> PSGI response, so from the
application's point of view, calling these methods still simply ends the
request with a redirect, as it does with the other framework plugins.

Note that this means these two methods must not be called from inside a
C<eval> / C<try> block that would swallow the exception without
re-throwing it.

=head2 Session

This middleware relies on C<< $env->{'psgix.session'} >> for the default
C<session> storage mode (see C<store_mode> in L<OIDC::Client::Config>), so
L<Plack::Middleware::Session> (or an equivalent middleware populating this
key) must be enabled B<before> C<Plack::Middleware::OIDC> :

  builder {
    enable 'Session';
    enable 'OIDC', config => $oidc_config;
    $app;
  };

The C<stash> storage mode is also supported ; the stash is a plain hashref
scoped to the current request (stored in C<< $env->{'oidc.stash'} >>), lost
once the request ends. This C<stash> storage mode should only be used for
an API which must validate the token in the C<Authorization> header
for each request (Resource Server).

=head2 Reverse proxy

When running behind a reverse proxy, L<Plack::Middleware::OIDC> expects
the PSGI environment to describe the public request URL.

The reverse proxy must therefore configure C<psgi.url_scheme>,
C<SERVER_NAME> and C<SERVER_PORT> appropriately before the OIDC middleware
is invoked.

L<Plack::Middleware::OIDC> does not interpret X-Forwarded-* headers itself.

Example (the order is important) :

  builder {
    enable 'ReverseProxy';
    enable 'Session';
    enable 'OIDC', config => $oidc_config;
    $app;
  };

=head1 CONFIGURATION

=head2 config

Hashref with the same structure as the configuration used by the other
framework plugins ; see L<OIDC::Client::Config> for the full reference.

=head2 log

Optional logger object, must implement the C<debug>, C<info>, C<error>
and (C<warning> or C<warn>) methods. Defaults to an instance of
L<Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Logger>, which writes to C<STDERR> (or to
C<< $env->{'psgix.logger'} >> when available).

=cut

=head1 METHODS

=head2 prepare_app()

Called once when the middleware is built. Depending on the configuration,
creates and keeps in memory one L<OIDC::Client> (stateless object) per
configured provider, and builds the internal table used to intercept the
callback routes.

=cut

sub prepare_app {
  my $self = shift;

  my $config = $self->config || {};

  $self->{_oidc_config} = $config;
  $self->{_oidc_log}    = $self->log || Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Logger->new();

  my (%client_by_provider, %path_map);

  foreach my $provider_name (keys %{ $config->{provider} || {} }) {
    my $config_provider = clone($config->{provider}{$provider_name} || {});
    $config_provider->{provider} = $provider_name;

    $client_by_provider{$provider_name} = OIDC::Client->new(
      config => $config_provider,
      log    => $self->{_oidc_log},
    );

    foreach my $action_type (qw/ login logout /) {
      my $path = $action_type eq 'login' ? $config_provider->{signin_redirect_path}
                                         : $config_provider->{logout_redirect_path}
        or next;

      if (my $existing_route = $path_map{$path}) {
        croak("OIDC: path '$path' is already used by provider '$existing_route->{provider}' "
            . "($existing_route->{action}), cannot also use it for provider '$provider_name' "
            . "($action_type)");
      }

      $path_map{$path} = { provider => $provider_name, action => $action_type };
    }
  }

  if ($config->{default_provider} && !$client_by_provider{$config->{default_provider}}) {
    croak("OIDC: default_provider '$config->{default_provider}' is not configured");
  }

  $self->{_oidc_client_by_provider} = \%client_by_provider;
  $self->{_oidc_path_map}           = \%path_map;

  return;
}


=head2 call( $env )

Called for every request. If the request path matches a configured
callback route, handles it directly. Otherwise, injects the
L<OIDC::Client::Plugin> instances into C<$env> and delegates to the
downstream application, converting any L<Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Redirect>
exception raised by the application into a real C<302> response.

=cut

sub call {
  my ($self, $env) = @_;

  if ($self->{_oidc_log}->isa('Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Logger')) {
    $self->{_oidc_log}->set_env($env);
  }

  if (my $route = $self->{_oidc_path_map}{ $env->{PATH_INFO} }) {
    return $route->{action} eq 'login' ? $self->_login_callback($env, $route->{provider})
                                       : $self->_logout_callback($env, $route->{provider});
  }

  $self->_inject_oidc_instances($env);

  return try {
    $self->app->($env);
  }
  catch {
    my $e = $_;
    if (blessed($e) && $e->isa('Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Redirect')) {
      $self->_response_from_redirect_exception($e)
    }
    else {
      die $e;
    }
  };
}


# Builds and injects one OIDC::Client::Plugin instance per configured
# provider into $env, plus the $env->{oidc} shortcut for the default one.
sub _inject_oidc_instances {
  my ($self, $env) = @_;

  my $default_provider_name = $self->_resolve_default_provider_name();

  foreach my $provider_name (keys %{ $self->{_oidc_client_by_provider} }) {
    my $plugin = $self->_build_plugin_instance($env, $provider_name);

    $env->{"oidc.$provider_name"} = $plugin;
    $env->{oidc} = $plugin
      if defined $default_provider_name && $provider_name eq $default_provider_name;
  }

  return;
}


sub _resolve_default_provider_name {
  my $self = shift;

  if (my $default_provider_name = $self->{_oidc_config}{default_provider}) {
    return $default_provider_name;
  }

  my @provider_names = keys %{ $self->{_oidc_client_by_provider} };

  return $provider_names[0] if @provider_names == 1;
  return undef;
}


sub _build_plugin_instance {
  my ($self, $env, $provider_name) = @_;

  my $client = $self->{_oidc_client_by_provider}{$provider_name}
    or croak("OIDC: no client for provider '$provider_name'");

  if ($client->store_mode eq 'session' && !$env->{'psgix.session'}) {
    croak("OIDC: no session found in \$env->{'psgix.session'} - "
        . "enable Plack::Middleware::Session (or an equivalent middleware) "
        . "before Plack::Middleware::OIDC");
  }

  $env->{'oidc.stash'} ||= {};

  my $req = Plack::Request->new($env);

  return OIDC::Client::Plugin->new(
    log             => $self->{_oidc_log},
    request_params  => $self->_extract_request_params($req),
    request_headers => $self->_extract_request_headers($req),
    session         => $env->{'psgix.session'} || {},
    stash           => $env->{'oidc.stash'},
    redirect        => sub { die Plack::Middleware::OIDC::Redirect->new(url => $_[0]); },
    client          => $client,
    base_url        => $req->base->as_string,
    current_url     => $req->uri->as_string,
  );
}


sub _extract_request_params {
  my ($self, $req) = @_;

  # Hash::MultiValue->keys returns one entry per value (duplicates for
  # multi-valued keys), so unique them first
  my $parameters = $req->parameters;
  my %seen_key;
  my @unique_keys = grep { !$seen_key{$_}++ } $parameters->keys;

  my %params;
  foreach my $key (@unique_keys) {
    my @values = $parameters->get_all($key);
    $params{$key} = @values > 1 ? \@values
                                : $values[0];
  }

  return \%params;
}


sub _extract_request_headers {
  my ($self, $req) = @_;

  my %headers;
  $req->headers->scan(sub { $headers{ $_[0] } = $_[1] });

  return \%headers;
}


sub _response_from_redirect_exception {
  my ($self, $exception) = @_;

  return [302, ['Location' => $exception->url], ['']];
}


# code executed on callback after authentication attempt
sub _login_callback {
  my ($self, $env, $provider_name) = @_;

  my $req     = Plack::Request->new($env);
  my $state   = $req->parameters->get('state');
  my $session = $env->{'psgix.session'};

  # oidc_auth is routing/application state. # OIDC::Client::Plugin::get_token() remains responsible
  # for validating the OIDC state/nonce/PKCE flow.
  my $auth_data = ($state && $session) ? $session->{oidc_auth}{$state}
                                       : undef;

  my $plugin = $self->_build_plugin_instance(
    $env,
    ($auth_data && $auth_data->{provider}) || $provider_name,
  );

  return try {
    $plugin->get_token();
    $self->_redirect_response($env, $auth_data && $auth_data->{target_url});
  }
  catch {
    my $e = $_;
    $self->_handle_authentication_error($env, $e);
  };
}


# code executed on callback after user logout
sub _logout_callback {
  my ($self, $env, $provider_name) = @_;

  my $req     = Plack::Request->new($env);
  my $state   = $req->parameters->get('state');
  my $session = $env->{'psgix.session'};

  my $logout_data = ($state && $session) ? delete $session->{oidc_logout}{$state}
                                         : undef;

  my $plugin = $self->_build_plugin_instance(
    $env,
    ($logout_data && $logout_data->{provider}) || $provider_name,
  );
  $plugin->delete_stored_data();

  return $self->_redirect_response($env, $logout_data && $logout_data->{target_url});
}


sub _redirect_response {
  my ($self, $env, $target_url) = @_;

  my $req = Plack::Request->new($env);

  return [302, ['Location' => $target_url || $req->base->as_string], ['']];
}


sub _handle_authentication_error {
  my ($self, $env, $e) = @_;

  die $e
    unless blessed($e) && $e->isa('OIDC::Client::Error');

  my $e_path = $self->{_oidc_config}{authentication_error_path}
    or die $e;

  # equivalent of Mojolicious' flash: kept only for the request following
  # the redirect, via the 'oidc_flash' session key
  ($env->{'psgix.session'} ||= {})->{oidc_flash}{error_message} = $e->message;

  my $req   = Plack::Request->new($env);
  my $e_url = URI->new_abs($e_path, $req->base->as_string)->as_string;

  return [302, ['Location' => $e_url], ['']];
}


1;
